window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2016-05-24"]={"AD-013-16":"Resolution No. AD-013-16\nFrom: Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nDISTRIBUTION OF OCTOBER THROUGH DECEMBER 2015 CASINO REVENUE\nTO VARIO US MUNICIPALITIES\nWHEREAS, a Seneca Nation gambling casino governed by the Tribal-State Class III Gaming Compact and disbursing monies according to provisions of Subdivision 3-a of the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act continues to operate Class III gaming activities within the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, said casino is operated and owned by the Seneca Nation of Indians, a sovereign and foreign nation which is not subject to any taxation by the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, the Tribal-State Class III Gaming Compact and its authorizing legislation, provide for compensation for the \"host municipalities\" of the Seneca Niagara Casino and said Compact was submitted to and approved by the Secretary of the Interior of the United States as required, and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act, a special separate revenue account denominated \"Tribal-State Compact Revenue Account\" was established in the joint custody of the State Comptroller and the State Commissioner to receive the gaming revenues payable to the State and the host municipalities, and\nWHEREAS, per the provisions of Subdivision 3 of the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act, 25% of revenues arising from the Seneca Niagara Casino are disbursed to the City of Niagara Falls, and\nWHEREAS, per the provisions of the Update New York Gaming and Economic Development Act Subdivision 3-a, \"Ten Percent of any of the funds actually received by the state pursuant to the tribal-state compacts and agreements described in [subdivision 2 of the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act] prior to the h\u00b7ansfer of moneys to the general fund required by such subdivision, shall be distributed to counties in each respective exclusivity zone provided they do not otherwise receive a share of said revenues pursuant to [the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act]; Such distribution shall be made among such counties on a per capita basis, excluding the population of any municipality that receives a distribution pursuant to [Subdivision 3 of the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act],\" and\nWHEREAS, per the Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act Subdivision 3-a, revenues arising from the Seneca Niagara Casino that are disbursed to the County of Niagara are to be app011ioned by population for such purposes as shall benefit the residents of the Towns of Cambria, Hartland, Lewiston, Lockport, Newfane, Niagara, Pendleton, Porter, Royalton, Somerset, Wheatfield and Wilson, and the Cities of Lockport and North Tonawanda, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Treasurer's Office and the Niagara County Office of Management and Budget jointly established two interest-bearing revenue accounts, which shall be designated \"Niagara County Community Development Fund 1\" and \"Niagara County Tax Relief Fund,\" with all revenues appropriated to the county under Upstate New York Gaming and Economic Development Act Subdivision 3-a divided and deposited into said accounts, with 25% of said monies being deposited in Niagara County Community Development Fund 1 and 75% of said monies being deposited in Niagara County Tax Relief Fund, with these percentages of distribution remaining in force until December 31, 2015, and\nWHEREAS, in December of 2014, this body did vote on a resolution creating both the Niagara County Community Development Fund and the Niagara County Tax Relief Fund, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has received $164,728.37 for the period of October 1, 2015 through December 31, 2015 towards the Niagara County Tax Relief Fund, and will distribute the funds to the respective municipalities per the aforementioned fo1mula, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby approve the disbursement of the balance in the Niagara County Tax Relief Fund of $114,638.27 for the period of October 1, 2015 through December 31, 2015, inclusive of necessary prior period adjustments, to the following municipalities within the County of Niagara:\nTOWNS  Cambria  $ 4,025.67\nHartland  $ 2,838.45\nLewiston  $12,006.01\nLockport  $14,153.63\nNewfane  $ 6,664.18\nNiagara  $ 5,776.17\nPendleton  $ 4,410.38\nPoiter  $ 4,668.24\nRoyalton  $ 5,281.15\nSomerset  $ 1,835.30\nWheatfield  $12,490.68\nWilson  $ 4,131.85\nCITIES  Lockport  $14,592.11\nNmth Tonawanda  $21 ,764.45\nand be it futther\nRESOLVED, that the County Treasurer be, and herby is, directed to pay the Supervisors of the various towns and city Treasurers the amounts recorded above and that this document shall be sufficient authorization to the County Treasurer to make the payments in accordance with the above direction.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vi1tuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","AD-014-16":"Resolution No. AD-014-16\nFrom: Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nNIAGARA COUNTY COMMUNITY COLLEGE LEARNING COMMONS PROJECT\nWHEREAS, by resolution dated June 16, 2015 and numbered IL-078-15 of the Niagara County Legislature, the Legislature formally committed to fund its matching share of $10,000,000 to the cost of the Niagara County Community College \"Learning Commons\" project, and\nWHEREAS, resolution AD-008-16 approved the issuance of debt up to $10,000,000 to finance the County's portion of the cost of the improvements to Niagara County Community College \"Learning Commons\" project, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Community College has already begun the planning of this construction using the College's share of the funding and has agreed to complete all requests for proposals and bids necessary and oversee the administration and completion of the project, and\nWHEREAS, the College has agreed to submit monthly progress reports to the County Deputy Commissioner of Engineering, and\nWHEREAS, a capital project is necessary to track payments made to the College, and verify the total project cost to the County does not exceed the $10,000,000 authorized by this Legislature, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Treasurer is authorized to remit payments to NCCC during the life of the project as deemed appropriate by the County Treasurer as _percentage of completion milestones are met, as reviewed and approved by the County Deputy Commissioner of Engineering for the construction of the Learning Commons, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following capital project be created with funding from the bonds previously authorized, and that the following budget modification be effectuated: INCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH634.15.2497.000 45710.16  Serial Bonds Year 2016  $10,000,000\nINCREASE ANTIC IP A TED APPROPRIATION:\nH634.15.2497.000 72200.02  NCCC Buildings  $10,000,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vhtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","AD-015-16":"Resolution No. AD-015-16\nFrom: Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nCONVERSION OF REFUSE DISPOSAL DISTRICT FROM A PROPRIETARY ENTERPRISE\nTYPE FUND TO A GOVERNMENT AL FUND\nWHEREAS, the Refuse Disposal District was created by Legislative Order dated January 21, 1969, and\nWHEREAS, the Refuse Disposal District has historically been treated as a proprietary, enterprise type fund due to the original nature of its business as an operating landfill site, and\nWHEREAS, Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) and the Governmental Accounting Standards Board (GASB) state that proprietary funds are used to account for a government's activities that are similar to businesses found in the private sector, are considered self-supp01ting in that the services rendered are by them are generally financed through user charges or on a cost reimbursement basis. Proprietary enterprise type funds are used to account for any activity for which a fee is charged to external users for good or services, in the context of the activity's principal revenue sources, and\nWHEREAS, the District discontinued operations as an active landfill in June of2013 by resolution PW-071-13 and has since been primarily funded by prope1ty taxes, and\nWHEREAS, the County's independent auditors recommended via the 2014 audit management letter to establish a governmental, special district fund in which to account for the transactions of the Refuse Disposal District, therefore be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature does hereby approve the conversion of the Refuse Disposal District from a proprietary enterprise type fund to a governmental fund.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vi1tuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","AD-016-16":"Resolution No. AD-016-16\nFrom: Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nA LOCAL LAW INCREASING THE SALARY OF\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPUTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY\nWHEREAS, the Administration Committee presents in writing the following proposed Local Law: A Local Law of the County of Niagara, New York for the year 2016 increasing the salary of the Niagara County Deputy District Attorney; Be enacted by the County Legislature of the County of Niagara, as follows: 1. During the term of a vacancy in the office of the Niagara County District Attorney, the attorney designated to perform the functions and duties of the Niagara County District Attorney during such vacancy shall receive the same compensation as the elected Niagara County District Attorney would have been entitled to receive.\n2.  On March 22, 2016, the date of the resignation of the Niagara County District Attorney, the salary\nestablished for the Niagara County District Attorney was $152,500.00; effective April 1, 2016 the salary of the Niagara County District Attorney was increased to $183,400.00 by operation of law due to the increases in salaries of the County Court Judges of the State of New York. [See Judiciary Law 183-a]; 3. Prior to his resignation, the elected Niagara County District Attorney designated a particular Niagara County Deputy District Attorney to perform the functions and duties of the Niagara County District Attorney during his absence and/or a vacancy in such office, therefore, such designated Niagara County Deputy District Attorney shall receive a salary of $152,500.00, effective March 22, 2016; and effective April 1, 2016 the salary of the Niagara County District Attorney shall be increased to $183,400.00 during the period of a vacancy in the office of the Niagara County District Attorney. This Local Law shall become effective in accordance with the statutes of the State of New York.\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature shall conduct a public hearing upon said proposed Local law at the Legislative Chambers, Comthouse, Lockport, New York on the 21 51 day of June, 2016 at 6:30 p.m., and\nbe it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature, at least six (6) days in advance of such hearing, shall post a notice upon the bulletin boards in the Courthouse in Lockport, the Civic Building in Niagara Falls shall publish such notice once in the Union Sun & Journal, and the Niagara Gazette; such notice shall contain the title of the Local Law and an abstract of the text to be prepared by the Clerk of the Legislatme, with the assistance of the County Attorney's Office\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","AD-017-16":"Resolution No. AD-017-16\nFrom: Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nSUMMER HOURS - 2016\nWHEREAS, the County has agreed to a ten-week period for a summer work schedule change and,\nWHEREAS, it was agreed that this new schedule will apply only to those departments and employees who are on a Monday through Friday, 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. schedule, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that effective June 26, 2016 through September 3, 2016 the work schedule of the County employees mentioned above will be 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. with the following regulations:\nI.  Any department required by local regulations or law to remain open until 5:00 p.m. may be\nexempt from this schedule.\n2.  Each Department Head is authorized to return to the 9:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. schedule if the new\nhoms cause operational problems, or if the employees abuse the new work schedule.\n3.  This agreement is for the ten-week period as stated above and will not be repeated without\nfurther Legislative action.\n4.  Public notice of the hours will be posted in all County offices affected.\n5.  Any deviation from the above must be cleared with the Human Resomces Department.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vi1tuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","CS-023-16":"Resolution No. CS-023-16\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees\nDated: May 24, 2016\nPUBLIC HEARING FOR\nNCCC FY2016-2017 MAINTENANCE AND OPERATING BUDGET\nWHEREAS, the County Manager and Budget Director have filed with the Legislative Clerk the tentative budget for the Niagara County Community College for their fiscal year commencing September 1, 2016 and ending August31,2017,and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to Section 359 of the County Law, a public hearing must be held, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that a public hearing be held in the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York at 6:45 p.m. on the 21st day of June, 2016 to review the tentative budget, and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature is hereby authorized and directed to publish notice of same in the Union Sun & Jomnal and the Niagara Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vittuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","CS-024-16":"Resolution No.CS-024-16\nFrom: Community Service and Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nPISTOL PERMIT FEE SCHEDULE\nWHEREAS, the County Clerk acts as issuing agent for the State of New York and is responsible for the administration of over 31,000 Pistol Permits issued to Niagara County residents, and\nWHEREAS, the County Clerk collects and pays associated Pistol Permit fees into the county treasury, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that pursuant to Section 400 of New York State Penal Law the following updated fee schedule be\nadopted effective June 1, 2016:\nPermit Application  $10.00\nAmendment  $3.00\nPermit Re-Issue  $5.00\nPermit Duplicate  $5.00\nGunsmith License  $10.00\nDealer License  $10.00\nPistol Permit Photo (Per Photo)  $5.00\nTransfer Out  $5.00\nTransfer In  $5.00\nPlastic Card  *$2.00\n*Applies to all plastic card transactions\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","CS-025-16":"Resolution No. CS-025-16\nFrom: Administration Committee\nDated: May 24, 2016\nUS PASSPORT PHOTO INCREASE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Clerk acts as the US Passport Acceptance Agent for the United States Department of State, and\nWHEREAS, the Clerk's office provides the convenience of taking passport photos for customers who are applying for or renewing their US Passport, and\nWHEREAS, all trained staff have successfully completed the annual certification necessary to maintain our ce1iification in service to our residents, and\nWHEREAS, the cost of consumables necessary to process and provide photos has increased, and\nWHEREAS, the fee for US Passpmi photos has remained the same for over a decade, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Clerk increase the fee charged for passpo1i photos from the cmTent fee of $8.00 to $10.00, effective June 1, 2016.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vhiuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent- Godfrey and Steed","CSS-033-16":"Resolution No. CSS-033-16\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees\nDated: May 24, 2016\nACCEPTANCE OF GUN INVOLVED VIOLENCE ELIMINATION\nINITIATIVE GRANT & BUDGET MODIFICATION\nWHEREAS, in 2014 the Division of Criminal Justice Services initiated a gun violence eradication initiative to target the rising incidents of gun involved violence across the State of New York and specifically the seventeen counties that account for the vast majority of crime outside of the City of New York which includes the County of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, the GIVE (Gun Involved Violence Elimination) Initiative is a statewide comprehensive strategy which was built on the successes of Operation IMP ACT but now focuses exclusively on reducing shootings and homicides by promoting integrated, evidence based strategies, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Niagara County Probation Department, in conjunction with the Niagara Falls Police Depa1tment, applied for the renewal of funding under the 2016-2017 GIVE Initiative, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County District Attorney's Office, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Niagara County Probation Depaitment have received notification that funding has been awai\u00b7ded in the amounts of $189,280, $101,065 and $56,907 respectively, for the period of July 1, 2016 through June 30, 2017, with no required match from Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office has a Correction Officer position #10442 which is cunently funded under this program and will remain coterminous with grant funding, and\nWHEREAS, the District Attorney's Office has an Assistant District Attorney position #4771 which is currently funded under this program and will remain coterminous with grant funding, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify revenue and appropriations in the District Attorney's Office, the Sheriffs Office and Probation Deprutment's budgets for fiscal year 2016, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara does hereby accept this award, and be it fuither\nRESOLVED, that prior to the execution of the grant award contract, the County Attorney will review the grant award contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chahman of the Niagara County Legislature be, and is hereby authorized to execute the grant award documents, and be it ftnther\nRESOLVED, that effective immediately, the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCM.02.1989. 115.43389.13  Crime Prevention  $40,678\nA.18.3140.000.43310.04  NYSDCJS  7,000\nA.17.3110.000.43389.13  Crime Prevention  1,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCM.02.1989.115.74300.01  Travel Conference  $1,500\nCM.02.1989.115.74650.08  Consultants/Expert Svcs.  39,178\nA.18.3140.000.71050.00  Ove1time  6,500\nA.18.3140.000.74600.03  Training/Education  500\nA.17.3110.000.74600.03  Training/Education  1,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Viltuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","CSS-034-16":"Resolution No. CSS-034-16\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees\nDated: May 24, 2016\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nNYS CANAL CORP. MARINE PATROL PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office was awarded a grant from the New York State Canal Corporation in the amount of $40,000 for the period April 1, 2016 through March 31, 2017, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will be used to patrol the canal to help facilitate new and existing local canal public safety patrols, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office will provide additional patrols on an overtime basis to meet the requirements of this grant, and\nWHEREAS, that prior to the execution of the grant award contract, the County Attorney will review the grant award contract for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be made:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000.71050.00  Overtime  $25,326\nA.17.3110.000.74750.21  Gas/Oil  6,000\nA.17.3110.000. 78100.00  Retirement  6,610\nA.17.3110.000. 78200.00  FICA  1,937\nA.17.3110.000.78300.00  Workers Compensation  127\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nA.17.3110.000.43315.00  Marine Patrol  $40,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vhtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","CSS-035-16":"Resolution No.CSS-035-16\nFrom: Community Safety & Security & Administration Committees\nDated: May 24, 2016.\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nTACTICAL TEAM TARGETED GRANT\nWHEREAS, Resolution CSS-014-16 accepted the Tactical Team Targeted grant in the amount of $100,000 as awarded by the New York State Deprutment of Homeland Security and Emergency Services, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are to be used to enhance the skills of the Sheriffs Office Emergency Response Team by providing additional skills and abilities to remain safe and successful in their mission, and\nWHEREAS, grant funds were originally appropriated for personnel overtime for training of the ERT team, however, after review of the cost and the logistics of sending employees for training, it would be beneficial to have the training done at the Sheriffs Office saving time and travel, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be made to pay for the trainer to train the ERT Team at the Niagara County Sheriff's Office:\nFROM:\nA.17.3110.71050.00  Ove1time  $7,100\nTO:\nA.17.3110.74600.03  Training and Education  $7,100\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Vittuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","ED-005-16":"Resolution No.ED-005-16\nFrom: Legislator Kathryn L. Lance, Economic Development and Administration Committees\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION AUTHORIZING NIAGARA COUNTY TO PROVIDE THE NIAGARA FALLS\nINTERNATIONAL AIRPORT STAKEHOLDERS GROUP, INC. REIMBURSEMENT FUNDS TO\nCOMPLETE NFIA BUSINESS DISTRICT FIBER-OPTIC NETWORK IMPLEMENTATION PLAN\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Falls International Airport Stakeholders Group Inc. (NFIASG) received $250,000 in grant funds to complete the Niagara County Broadband Assessment and Fiber-Optic Network Development Plan for the Niagara Falls International Airpmt Focus Area in 2015, and\nWHEREAS, the NFIASG will seek $90,000 in grant funding to conduct Phase II of the above-referenced project, the NFIA Business District Fiber-Optic Network Implementation Plan, from the New York Power Authority's (NYPA) Western New York Economic Development Fund, and\nWHEREAS, the NFIASG will also seek to use $90,000 in remaining funds it was awarded by National Grid through their Strategic Economic Development Outreach Program, for completion of the same NFIA Business District Fiber-Optic Network Implementation Plan, and\nWHEREAS, the NFIASG will seek to retain the services of Magellan Advisors LLC to complete the NFIA Business District Fiber-Optic Network Implementation Plan at a cost of $180,000, and\nWHEREAS, the National Grid grant is a reimbursement only grant program, as stipulated in the grant agreement executed between National Grid and the NFIASG, whereby funding is reimbursed only upon completion of the project and payment of all project expenses, and\nWHEREAS, grant funding through the Western New York Economic Development Fund may be disbursed directly upon encumbrance of project expenses or reimbursed upon payment of project expenses at the sole discretion of the NYP A, which will be stipulated in the grant agreement executed between the NFIASG and NYPA, and\nWHEREAS, the NFIASG requires funding to pay for project expenses in advance of receiving reimbursement from grantors and is seeking an amount not to exceed $180,000 in advanced funding from Niagara County, which would be the maximum amount requested if NYP A requires a reimbursement process for grant proceeds to pay for Phase II work to be completed by Magellan Advisors LLC, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that authorization is hereby granted to provide the NFIASG an\u00b7 amount not to exceed $180,000 in reimbursement funding, the actual amount of which will be stipulated in an invoice provided by the NFIASG, to pay for Phase II work to be completed by Magellan Advisors LLC, and be it futther,\nRESOLVED, that the NFIASG agrees to reimburse Niagara County the amount of funding it receives from the County upon the NFIASG receiving grant reimbursement funds from the NYP A and/or National Grid to ensure there is no cost incurred by the County, and be it futther\nRESOLVED, that the funds to be provided to the NFIASG be taken from and reimbursed to the following budget line: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.000 42189.01  Other Home & Comm Svc Income\nActivities - Economic Dev  $180,000 (maximum)\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.000 74500.01  Contractual Expenses  $180,000 (maximum)\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Virtuoso.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-026-16":"Resolution No.IL-026-16\nFrom: Legislator Richard L. Andres and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE RAILROAD MUSEUM OF THE NIAGARA FRONTIER\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New York State Finance Law \u00a7 99-h, and\nWHEREAS, communities in Niagara County are always looking for ways to attract visitors whose monies are needed to sustain the economy, and it bears noting that the tourism sector is now ranked as the 4th largest employer in New York State, and\nWHEREAS, as a component of New York State's $100 billion tourism industry, history tourism is a vital tool for economic development, serving to draw visitors to historic business districts of our communities, and\nWHEREAS, tourism centered on the western terminus of the Erie Canal drew more than 1 million visitors in 2014, with a substantial portion drawn by the canal's role in transportation history, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Frontier Chapter of the National Railway Historical Society has established the Railroad Museum of the Niagara Frontier at the Erie Railroad freight station in North Tonawanda to convey the rich history of railroads in our area, and\nWHEREAS, the Railroad Museum of the Niagara Frontier has sought to establish adequate signage to draw railroad history enthusiasts to their location, but is in need of funds to support that effoti, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County disburses \u00a7 99-h funding as follows:\nNiagara Frontier Chapter, National Railway Historical Society  $3,000.00\nand be it fu11her\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $3,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $3,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Syracuse.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-027-16":"Resolution No.IL-027-16\nFrom: Legislator Richard L. Andres and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF CARNEGIE ART CENTER GALLERY HANGING SYSTEM\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Carnegie Art Center, has served the Community of North Tonawanda for over 100 years, initially as Nmth Tonawanda's First Library, the Carnegie Library, beginning in 1904 and continuing until 1976,and\nWHEREAS, the Carnegie Att Center promotes and encourages the development of the ruts through exhibits, events and classes, including Student Spotlight- North Tonawanda City School District's Art Show, and\nWHEREAS, funding suppmt, membership and donations are key to the continuation of public access for over 31,000 people each year to the building and the creation of revenue generating programming that will support the future of the organization, and\nWHEREAS, the Carnegie Art Center, operated by the Tonawandas' Council on the Arts, is in need of a gallery hanging system to accommodate future showcase world class art exhibits, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Casino Funds help support some of the cost incurred by the purchase of a new gallery hanging system, and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County suppo1ts the Carnegie Arts Center as follows:\nThe Cru\u00b7negie Arts Center  $3,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $3,000.00\nINCREASED APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $3,000.00\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Hill.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-028-16":"Resolution No.IL-028-16\nFrom: Legislator John Syracuse and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE OLCOTT PIRATE'S FESTIVAL\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Pirates Festival was established in 1998 by the Olcott Fire Department, and\nWHEREAS, the Pirates Festival has been a mainstay in Niagara County's tourism offerings in the Town of Newfane and Eastern Niagara County, increasing the foot traffic in Olcott by nearly 16,000 people during the festival and bringing significant economic impact to the community, and\nWHEREAS, the 2016 Pirate's Festival is scheduled to take place from July 81h through July l01h at Krull Park in picturesque Olcott, New York, and\nWHEREAS, the festival offers pirate-themed rides, food vendors, music and live ente1iainment for the whole family to enjoy, and is well known for its premiere events; the Pirate's Parade and the Car Show, and\nWHEREAS, this free, family-friendly event brings thousands of visitors to the area and creates a positive benefit for local restaurants, shops and various other business owners and has a positive impact on all of Niagara County in the form of sales tax revenues, and\nWHEREAS, funds are being sought to help offset the costs associated with hosting a festival of this magnitude, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the economic initiatives of the Pirate's Festival as follows:\nPirate's Festival - Rental Fees  $3,500.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $3,500.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $3,500.00\nMoved by Syracuse, seconded by Wydysh.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-029-16":"Resolution No.IL-029-16\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE lllSTORICAL SOCIETY OF \"DAS HAUS\" NORTH GERMAN\nSETTLEMENTS THROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Historical Society ofN01th Geiman Settlements has maintained the \"Das Haus\" museum in the community of Bergholz, located in the Town of Wheatfield, and\nWHEREAS, the Historical Society of No1th German Settlements is very excited for the grand opening of the latest building addition, the \"EinHaus\", and\nWHEREAS, the Historical Society has this and other positive things happening at the museum, it has been found they need to re-do all their marketing materials, and\nWHEREAS, the paitial funding would be of help towards the update of new marketing materials, such as tri-fold brochures, business cards, letterhead, signage and other materials, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County Casino Funds help support some of the cost incurred to update marketing materials, and be it fu1iher\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County suppo1is the Historical Society of North Geiman Settlements as follows:\nThe Historical Society ofN01ih German Settlements  $2,000.00\nand be it fmiher\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $2,000.00\nINCREASED APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $2,000.00\nMoved by Wydysh seconded by Nemi.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-030-16":"Resolution No.IL-030-16\nFrom: Legislators Randy R. Bradt, Richard L. Andres, Kathryn L. Lance, David E. Godfrey, Rebecca J.Wydysh and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF FUNDING NIAGARA ERIE YOUTH SPORTS ASSOCIATION\nAED PROVISION THROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDS\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New York State statue, and\nWHEREAS, \"quality of life\" is a critical component of economic development strategy, with \"Area Development\" magazine noting in a January 2009 article that \"While companies seldom base their site-selection decisions solely on quality-of-life issues - housing, schools, healthcare, amenities, crime - these factors do play an increasingly imp011ant role in this decision-making process, especially for those dependent on the talents of highly educated workers,\" and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County supp011s those activities designed to enhance quality of life for residents as a critical facet in convincing businesses to locate high-wage jobs in this county, and deems a \"family-friendly\" environment essential to the economic development of the Westem New York region, and\nWHEREAS, youth athletics have long been a critical part of the appeal of municipalities in this county as\n\"bedroom communities\" for the local workforce, and  \"\nWHEREAS, the Niagara Erie Youth Sp011s Association administrates a 16-club, four weight-division youth football league in Niagara and Erie counties, and is responsible for the health and safety of youth athletes aged 6 to 14, as well as spectators and support personnel at NEYSA football games, and\nWHEREAS, according to inf01mation provided by the American Heart Association, 56% of cardiac-anest deaths in athletes were attributable to cardiovascular disease, with 82% of those deaths occuning as a result of physical exertion during competition or training, and a National Collegiate Athletic Association study of high school and college-aged athletes found an incidence of cardiac aITest of 1 per 22,903 athlete participant years, and that the survival rate among children with non-traumatic cardiac arrest is just 7.8%, and\nWHEREAS, a Harvard study indicated that utilization of an Automated External Defibrillator (AED) in combination with cardiopulmonary resuscitation increased the survival rate to 23%, and\nWHEREAS, adequate safety equipment is essential to the management of youth athletics and to lower liabilities, and\nWHEREAS, NEYSA has received a quoted price of $5,250 to acquire 6 AED devices, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports youth athletics and specifically wishes to see the Niagara Erie Youth \u00b7 Sports Association's regional football league continue to provide a positive outlet for youth that enhances quality of life for families residing in the Western New York region, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the above initiative by funded with monies as follows:\nNEYSA AED Acquisition  $5,250.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.02  Appropriated Fund Balance $5,250.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.08  Seneca Niagara Monies  $5,250.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Andres.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-031-16":"Resolution No.IL-031-16\nFrom: Legislator and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF LAKE ONTARIO VFW POST 313\nTHROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDS\nWHEREAS, the Lake Ontario VFW Post 313 is planning and event on this Memorial Day weekend as a tribute to our armed forces during the Vietnam War, and\nWHEREAS, it is important that we never forget the sacrifices made by our United States soldiers in the fight for freedom anywhere in the world, including the loss of their lives, and\nWHEREAS, the VFW has partnered with the American Huey 369 Museum Association from Peru, Indiana to bring two Bell UH-I (Huey) helicopters to the Old Fort Niagara State Park in Youngstown, N.Y. on Memorial Day weekend, May 28 - 29, 2016, and\nWHEREAS, the public will be able to actually take a once in a lifetime flight on these helicopters as well as witness a re-enactment of the 251h Infantry Division who served in Vietnam, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is proud of our servicemen who served and wants to do its part to honor them, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the above initiative by funded with monies as follows:\nLake Ontario VFW Post 313 Helicopter Expenses  $2,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.02  Appropriated Fund Balance $2,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.08  Seneca Niagara Monies  $2,000.00\nMoved by Bmmaster seconded by Hill.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-032-16":"Resolution No.IL-032-16\nFrom: Legislators Jason Zona and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF TOWN OF NIAGARA VETERANS MEMORIAL PARK\nTHROUGH USE OF CASINO FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per NYS Finance Law 99h, and\nWHEREAS, the legislature is committed to utilizing these funds for economic and community development efforts that will market our county to new businesses looking to expand or relocate, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Niagara hosts the largest producer of sales tax revenue in Niagara County with the Fashion Outlets of Niagara, and\nWHEREAS, it is proven quality of life is a critical component of economic development strategies, which includes local parks and recreation areas, which influence a company's decision to relocate in a specific area, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Niagara Veterans Memorial Park is in need of benches for the walking path that tuns from the Community Center, and through the remainder of the wooded walking area throughout the park, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the Niagara County Legislature supp01is the Town of Niagara Veterans Memorial Park, with the purchase of (6) six park benches for the walking path through the use of casino revenues, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the sum of $4,200 be allotted to the Town of Niagara for this worthwhile community development purchase, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the 2016 Niagara County economic development budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $4,200.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $4,200.00\nMoved by Zona seconded by Grozio\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-035-16":"Resolution No.IL-035-16\nFrom: Legislators Anthony J. Nemi, Wm. Keith McNall, William J. Collins and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF OUTWATER PARK BASEBALL DIAMOND\nIMPROVEMENTS THROUGH THE USE OF CASINO FUNDS\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New York State statute, and\nWHEREAS, \"quality of life\" is a critical component of economic development strategy, with \"Area Development\" magazine noting in a January 2009 ruticle that \"While companies seldom base their site-selection decisions solely on quality-of-life issues - housing, schools, healthcru\u00b7e, amenities, crime - these factors do play an increasingly important role in this decision-making process, especially for those dependent on the talents of highly educated workers,\" and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County supports those activities designed to enhance quality of life for residents as a critical facet in convincing businesses to locate high-wage jobs in this county, and deems a \"family-friendly\" environment essential to the economic development of the Western New York region, and\nWHEREAS, youth athletics have long been a critical part of the appeal of municipalities in this county as \"bedroom communities\" for the regional workforce, and\nWHEREAS, the City and Town of Lockp01t have long served as a hub for tournament baseball, principally through the Anjo baseball fields located adjacent to county prope1ties on Davison Road, and\nWHEREAS, those baseball diamonds located at Anjo Field are not built to the specifications set out in the Official Baseball Rules, the legal guidelines governing Major League Baseball and most large-scale tournament play in the Eastern U.S., but rather are built to Little League Baseball standru\u00b7ds, with 60 feet between bases, and\nWHEREAS, the baseball diamond at Outwater Park, also located in the City of Lockpo1t, and home to Lockport High School baseball as well as vrufous Western New York tournament teams playing in lru\u00b7ge-scale tournaments throughout the Eastern U.S. and Southern Canada, is in fact constructed to the specifications set out in the Official Baseball Rules, and\nWHEREAS, the refurbishment of said diamond at Outwater Park would provide additional incentive for scheduling tournament baseball games at that facility, and\nWHEREAS, the cost to rebuild the home plate and pitcher's mound, widen the base paths, and laser groom the remainder of the field has been bid at $17,088.00, and other entities including the City of Lockport Parks Department and the Lockport Board of Education have committed to partially funding the proposed upgrades,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supp011s youth athletics and specifically wishes to see the baseball diamond at Outwater Park upgraded and improved so it might continue to provide a positive outlet for youth and adults that enhances quality oflife for families residing in the Western New York region, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the above initiative by funded with monies as follows:\nOutwater Park baseball diamond upgrades  $10,000.00\nand be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2016 budget: INCREASE REVENUE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance  $10,000.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.812 74400.15  Seneca Niagara Community Development Fund  $10,000.00\nMoved by Nemi seconded by Collins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-036-16":"Resolution No.IL-036-16\nFrom: Legislators Anthony J. Nemi, Wm. Keith McNall, William J. Collins, Michael A. Hill, et al. and Economic Development\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT FUNDING\nFOR NIAGARA COUNTY GOLF PROGRAM\nWHEREAS, Niagara County is in receipt of funds generated from the Seneca Niagara Casino, as per New Yark State Law \u00a799-h, and\nWHEREAS, 2016 marks the 55th Anniversary of the Niagara County Golf Course, and\nWHEREAS, the Legislature wishes to honor this historic milestone, and to encourage the development of our youth in this sport, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County benefits from the additional pa1ticipation of youth enjoying the Niagara County Golf Course, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of the Legislature to attract youth and encourage greater participation in the Niagara County Golf Course by promoting a County Golf Program, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby agrees to dedicate funds to the Niagara County Golf Course budget to help with costs for events, clinics, adve1tising and national organization fees, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Golf Course 2016 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.812 40599 .0 I  Appropriated Fund Balance-Committed Funds $4,800\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.07.9901 .000 790 I 0.00  Contribution to Other Funds  $4,800\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nER.26.9901 .000 45031.00  Interfund Transfer from Operating  $4,800\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nER.26.7140.000 74000.02  Miscellaneous Fees  $1,200\nER.26.7140.000 74375.01  Advertising  $1, 100\nER.26.7140.000 74500.01  Contractual  $2,000\nER.26.7140.000 74675.01  Postage  $500\nMoved by Nemi seconded by Collins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed","IL-039-16":"Resolution No.IL-039-16\nFrom: Legislators John Syracuse and William J. Collins\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT FOR THE CONTINUATION OF THE PROCESS OF STUDYING THE\nFORMATION OF THE NIAGARA COUNTY HEALTHCARE CONSORTIUM\nWHEREAS, the Greater Niagara Orleans County Municipal Health Insurance Cons01tium Study consists of twenty-six municipal entities throughout Niagara and Orleans Counties; and\nWHEREAS, the Conso1tium's goal is to reduce overall health care costs to municipalities through purchasing health insurance jointly, and\nWHEREAS, by consensus vote of the Conso1tium Ad Hoc Committee comprised of participating\nmunicipalities, it was directed that the Town of Somerset as lead agency for the participating members apply for a $250,000.00 grant from the New York State Department of State to further study the Consortium, and\nWHEREAS, the twelve towns in Niagara County, who are all paiticipating members, in this project, each contributed $417. 00 to date towards the cost of the grant writer to pursue the state grant, and\nWHEREAS, the Consortium was successful in that the state grant was awarded and the Town of Somerset was designated as the lead agency for administering the grant; and\nWHEREAS, as a condition to the Department of State $250,000.00 grant, the recipient must provide matching funds equal to I 0% of the overall grant total, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Somerset, as the lead agency, is responsible for providing the $25,000.00 in matching funds as required by the New York State Department of State, and\nWHEREAS, by consensus vote of the Consortium Ad Hoc Committee comprised of participating\nmunicipalities, it was agreed that participating municipalities would equally reimburse the Town of Somerset for the matching funds required to be paid by the Town of Somerset for this grant, and\nWHEREAS, at this time and during the grant process, there is no obligation of any municipality to join the Cons01tium upon its final implementation, and\nWHEREAS, in order to completely evaluate the full costs and benefits of a Cons01tium, it is essential that the grant project continue until its conclusion, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Somerset will seek reimbursement on a quarterly basis from the State for services and expenses rendered under this Grant, in which the State will deduct the matching grant p01tion, and\nWHEREAS, the Town of Somerset will then submit quarterly to each paiticipating municipality a voucher for its portion of the matching grant share, and\nWHEREAS, this municipality is one of the entities paiticipating in this project and will continue to benefit from the Consortium study and potential implementation, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that in the spirit of cooperation, this municipal body agrees that the cost of the matching amount borne by the Town of Somerset shall be allocated amongst the final list of pa1ticipating municipalities in equal shares, with each Niagara County town who contributed towards the cost of the initial grant writer receiving a $417.00 deduction from this equal share in order to ensure that each entity contributes an identical amount towards this w01thy project, and be it further\nRESOLVED, this municipal body does hereby authorize payment to reimburse the Town of Somerset in amounts not to exceed 288.50 in qua1terly reimbursement over a 2 year period for our share of the grant matching funds for this w01thy project, and be it fmther\nRESOLVED, that the final roster of participating entities will be determined by May 31st 2016, and this municipal body recognizes that the aforementioned equal share reimbursements will be adjusted accordingly; pursuant to the number of entities on the final roster.\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.13.1430.106 74500.01  Contractual Expense  577\nDECREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.08.1991.000 74500.01  General Government Support  577\nMoved by Syracuse, seconded by Hill, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to refenfog it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Syracuse, seconded by Hill.\nAdopted. 12 Ayes, 1Noes,2 Absent- Godfrey and Steed","IL-044-16":"Resolution No.IL-044-16\nFrom: Legislators Wm. Keith McNall, Kathryn L. Lance, Randy R. Bradt, et al.\nDated: May 24, 2016\nRESOLUTION ENDORSING NIAGARA COUNTY COMMUNITIES' APPLICATIONS FOR\nWESTERN NEW YORK REGIONAL ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT COUNCIL DOWNTOWN\nREVITALIZATION INITIATIVE GRANT APPLICATIONS\nWHEREAS, New York State's Regional Economic Development Councils are each holding a competitive application process for a $10 million Downtown Revitalization Initiative grant to be awarded to a single community in each region deemed \"ripe for development\" into \"vibrant communities where tomorrow's workforce will want to live, work and raise families,\" and\nWHEREAS, the Western New York Regional Economic Development, of which the Honorable Wm. Keith McNall, chairman of this Legislature, is a member, has issued a Downtown Revitalization Grant application, and\nWHEREAS, four Niagara County communities have indicated an intent to move forward with applications for the Western New York Regional Economic Development Council's Downtown Revitalization Initiative, and\nWHEREAS, the applications submitted by Niagara County communities are synopsized by those respective communities' economic development agencies as follows: City of Lockport: The City of Lockport's Downtown Revitalization Initiative builds upon the recent economic development successes in downtown Lockport, such as progress at HaITison Place, the continued restoration of the Flight of Five locks, and the construction of Comerstone CFCU Arena. The nomination request highlights the potential for an even greater impact on Lockp011, Niagara County, and throughout the Western New York region by enhancing existing assets in the City's downtown, like The Historic Palace Theatre and vacant and under-utilized mixed-use properties; City of Niagara Falls: The City of Niagara Falls is targeting the area north of Niagara Street to create a vibrant, year-round downtown district. Major projects like the Seneca Niagara Casino, expansion of Niagara Falls Memorial Medical Center, NCCC's Culinary Institute, construction/renovation/expansion of new hotels have been completed in recent years resulting in creation of new jobs. This trend will only continue as there are other projects in the pipeline to continue the diversification of the Niagara Falls economy. Projects like Housing Visions and the Market Rate Rental Rehabilitation program have begun to create an attractive livable community for diverse populations. The city's 2009 Master Plan has laid a framework of policies that will allow Niagara Falls to integrate continued growth through the DRI; City of North Tonawanda: The City of North Tonawanda's nomination for the Downtown Revitalization Initiative, known as the NT Momentum project, is the culmination of ten years of comprehensive planning focused on North Tonawanda's historic downtown. This initiative will catalyze development in North Tonawanda along the Niagara River and Erie Canal waterfronts anchored by a historic downtown filled with culture, arts, recreation, tourism, and a great atmosphere in which to live and work. Focused on leveraging strong local assets, this project will bring new investment to North Tonawanda and spark resurgence in downtown. The Downtown Revitalization Initiative is the opportunity to implement the NT Momentum project and bring the communities downtown redevelopment vision to reality; Transit North: The Transit N011h - Historic Canal way Corridor Downtown Revitalization Project brings together three Niagara County communities, the Towns of Lockport and Pendleton and the City Lockport, who are seeking to revitalize the six-mile Transit North coITidor which commences at Tonawanda Creek Road and terminates at the intersection of Main and Washburn Streets in the City of Lockport, and has average daily traffic counts of 29,250. The historic-themed retail shopping district will not only lead to the development of a gateway that creates a sense of aITival and a place w01ih remembering, but will also help drive business back onto Main Street in the City of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, it is the judgment of the Niagara County Center for Economic Development that these applications are of similar weight and value, and are in line with the vision for development set foith in the Niagara County Comprehensive Plan, and\nWHEREAS, this Legislature is prepared to supp011 implementation eff011s by any Niagara County community awarded the Downtown Revitalization Initiative grant, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara does hereby endorse the Downtown Revitalization Initiative grant applications of the City of Lockport, the City of Niagara Falls, the City of North Tonawanda, and the Transit North consortium, and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara does hereby direct Commissioner Sam Ferraro and the Center for Economic Development to provide any assistance or information sought by the Western New York Regional Economic Development Council in its effo11s to evaluate the strengths of the applications from the four Niagara County communities, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara does hereby direct Commissioner Sam Ferraro and the Center for Economic Development to provide full assistance to any Niagara County community that should be awarded the DRI funding as it implements the project outlined in its application, and be it fu1ther\nRESOLVED, that this Legislature directs that individual copies of this Resolution be sent to all representatives seated on the Western New York Regional Economic Development Council, namely Satish K. Tripathi, Jeff Belt, Crystal Abers, Aaron Baiiley, Robe11 T. Brady, Deanna Alterio Brennen, Byron W. Brown, Paul Brown, Curtis Crandall, Michael Cropp, Colleen C. DiPirro, Paul A. Dyster, Dr. Charles Edmondson, Vince Horrigan, Virginia Horvath, Dottie Gallagher-Cohen, Dr. Rosa Gonzalez, Pamela R. Henderson, John R. Koelmel, Thomas A. Kucharski, Brenda W. McDuffie, Wm. Keith McNall, Michael Metzger, Jennifer J. Parker, Mark Poloncarz, Senator Michael H. Ranzenhofer, Member of the Assembly Robin Schimminger, Samuel Teresi, and Melinda Vizcarra, and Empire State Development Corp. Western New York Regional Director Christopher Schoepflin, as well as representatives of the four Niagara County municipal economic development agencies submitting applications.\nMoved by Lance seconded by Bradt.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Godfrey and Steed"};
